# Legacy CMS URL repair. These sites are static exports of the old Omeka
# installs, and the export appended .html to every page: /items/show/60 became
# /items/show/60.html. Everything indexed or cited before the migration points
# at the extensionless form. Proven on occupyarchive.org: 272 redirects, 190
# hits.
#
# Ordering matters — Caddy runs `redir` before `handle`, so these fire ahead of
# the object-storage matcher, and among the `redir`s below file order decides.
# The specific rules must therefore stay above the generic one at the bottom.
@needs_html {
    not path *.*       # already has an extension: .html (no loop), assets
    not path */        # directories already resolve to index.html
    not path /files/*  # Garage-served; extensionless hashes must reach their handler
}

# On /files/*: the crawl excludes files/original|fullsize|thumbnails|
# square_thumbnails (see .gitignore), so those reach Garage through the
# not-found handler — which only runs once every `redir` has declined. This
# site's own file URLs all carry extensions, so `not path *.*` happens to cover
# them, but pandemicreligion.org, collectingthesetimes.org and
# www.preachinggoesviral.org serve /files/original/<hash> with no extension at
# all. That URL style and this rule cannot coexist: the 301 to <hash>.html wins
# and the object never gets served. Exclude the prefix outright rather than
# leave a landmine for whoever adds redirects to those sites next.

# Path-style pagination — /items/browse/2 — is what the logs still ask for, and
# the generic rule below turns it into /items/browse/2.html, which does not
# exist (33 requests across /1 … /17). The export holds those listings as
# items/browse?page=N.html; %3F is exactly how the exported pages link to one
# another, and the file server decodes it back to the literal `?` in the
# filename. Bounded to the 17 pages that were captured, so a stray higher
# number falls through to the generic rule instead of 301ing onto a missing
# file.
@browse_page path_regexp browsepage ^/items/browse/([1-9]|1[0-7])/?$
redir @browse_page /items/browse%3Fpage={re.browsepage.1}.html 301

# Anything else below /items/browse/ lands on the real browse page. This also
# covers the bare trailing slash, which `not path */` makes @needs_html skip
# altogether — it falls through to the file server, finds no items/browse/
# directory to serve, and 404s. A wildcard is safe here precisely because
# nothing lives under items/browse/ in this export; do not copy this line to
# mallhistory, where items/browse/type/*.html are real files.
@browse_other path /items/browse/ /items/browse/*
redir @browse_other /items/browse.html 301

# {path} drops the query, so every /items/browse?advanced[0][…] facet collapses
# to /items/browse.html, which this export did capture.
#
# NOT for Hugo sites — those need /foo/, not /foo.html, and this would 301 them
# into a 404.
redir @needs_html {path}.html 301
